      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190413/assets/1534153e6a5d79400a038849b48583d2ec9a62db1fc1f52a491b751e46861f51.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=0$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=0.$ Applying the above formula with $x=0$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(0) & ≈\\frac{g(0+2)-g(0)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(2)-g(0)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-10}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{2} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
